Ladybird now types each number in a text node by rules W2, W4, and W5 of
UAX #9, and always shapes its glyphs left to right. By rule W7, a number
goes into the line as part of the left-to-right text before it, if there
is any, and otherwise as part of the right-to-left text around it —
unless rule I2 puts it at the level of left-to-right text right next to
it. Blink, WebKit, and Gecko instead resolve the levels of a whole
paragraph with ICU’s ubidi: Blink in BidiParagraph::SetParagraph(),
WebKit in InlineItemsBuilder::breakAndComputeBidiLevels(), and Gecko in
mozilla::intl::Bidi::SetParagraph(). Chrome 157.0.8103.79 and Firefox
151.0 lay out all 41 paragraphs of the new test the way this change
does. WebKit 26.5 differs in three: two left-to-right paragraphs with
Hebrew or Arabic-Indic digits, and a plaintext paragraph. And since all
three engines resolve levels across the whole paragraph, they also get a
number split across elements or fonts right, which this change doesn’t.
